 Of Scope</b:DisplayName><b:Name>71f3d9aa-b8ef-4e54-8126-607a1d903103</b:Name><b:Value i:type="c:boolean" xmlns:c="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">false</b:Value></a:anyType><a:anyType i:type="b:StringDisplayAttribute" xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:DisplayName>Reason For Out Of Scope</b:DisplayName><b:Name>752473b6-52d4-4776-9a24-202153f7d579</b:Name><b:Value i:type="c:string" xmlns:c="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"/></a:anyType></Properties><TypeId xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">GE.A</TypeId><Height xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">100</Height><Left xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">511</Left><StrokeDashArray i:nil="true" xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts"/><StrokeThickness xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">1</StrokeThickness><Top xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">1149</Top><Width xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">431</Width></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfguidanyType><a:KeyValueOfguidanyType><a:Key>b0a69d5d-761a-40d9-bc97-593dadeb0e0a</a:Key><a:Value z:Id="i75" i:type="StencilRectangle"><GenericTypeId xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">GE.A</GenericTypeId><Guid xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">b0a69d5d-761a-40d9-bc97-593dadeb0e0a</Guid><Properties xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts"><a:anyType i:type="b:HeaderDisplayAttribute" xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:DisplayName>Free Text Annotation</b:DisplayName><b:Name/><b:Value i:nil="true"/></a:anyType><a:anyType i:type="b:StringDisplayAttribute" xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:DisplayName>Name</b:DisplayName><b:Name/><b:Value i:type="c:string" xmlns:c="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">The intent of this Diagram is to give a general overview and a launch point for a conversation about the security of our solution from the viewpoint of anend to end VM Create request as it is one of the most complex resources we manage.&#xD;
&#xD;
ALM VM Creation Flow&#xD;
====================&#xD;
1. Customer creates a VM using the az stack hci-vm CLI (or ARM API).&#xD;
2. A Hybrid Compute Machine resource is created in ARM to serve as the parent resource for the Azure Stack HCI virtual machine.&#xD;
3. An Azure Stack HCI virtual machine resource is created as an extension resource on the Hybrid Compute Machine.&#xD;
4. A VM Custom Resource (CR) is created in the ARB Kubernetes cluster via Resource Sync. The VM password is stored as a K8s secret referenced by the CR; the SSH public key is stored directly on the CR spec.&#xD;
5. The azlocal-overlay extension detects and reconciles the VM CR. The overlay reads the password secret and SSH key from the CR, then issues a CreateVirtualMachine() gRPC call to VMO over a TLS channel authenticated by JWT.&#xD;
6. VMO generates an ECDSA P-256 key pair for this VM and writes a base64-encoded JSON state file to cluster storage at ...\vmckeys\&lt;hyper-v-vm-id&gt;.state, containing the private key (for JWT signing) and public key (for VMC validation).&#xD;
7. VMO creates the Hyper-V VM object on the cluster node.&#xD;
8. VMO builds a customization ISO containing:&#xD;
   - Cloud-init user-data (Linux) or autounattend.xml (Windows) with the customer's credentials&#xD;
   - VMC binary (code-signed at build time)&#xD;
   - VMC install script&#xD;
   - The ECDSA P-256 public key (current.pub) for JWT validation&#xD;
   - A GUID tag file for ISO detection during rehoming&#xD;
9. VMO attaches the ISO to the VM and returns "VM created (not started)" to the overlay.&#xD;
10. The overlay issues a LaunchVirtualMachine() call. VMO enrolls the VM in cluster high-availability and starts the VM.&#xD;
11. The guest OS boots and runs cloud-init (Linux) or sysprep (Windows), consuming the customer's password and SSH key to configure the initial user account.&#xD;
12. The VMC install script runs, copying the VMC binary and public key to their runtime locations (/var/lib/vmc/keys/current.pub on Linux, C:\ProgramData\vmc\keys\current.pub on Windows) and starting VMC.&#xD;
13. VMC starts and generates its own TLS PKI -- a self-signed server certificate -- then begins listening on a Hyper-V socket with TLS.&#xD;
14. The VM operator generates an RSA-2048 key pair for Arc for Servers enrollment. The public key is written to the VM CR and synced to ARM via Resource Sync. The private key is stored in a K8s secret.&#xD;
15. The private key is passed to VMO via the overlay gRPC channel, and VMO forwards it to VMC via a JWT-authenticated Hyper-V socket call (GuestInstallArcForServers).&#xD;
16. VMC downloads and executes the Arc for Servers enrollment script, running azcmagent connect existing with the RSA private key to register the guest VM with Azure Arc, connecting it to the Hybrid Compute Machine created in step 2.&#xD;
17. The Azure Arc agent establishes ongoing communication with Hybrid Compute and Hybrid Identity across the Microsoft Cloud boundary.</b:Value></a:anyType><a:anyType i:type="b:BooleanDisplayAttribute" xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:DisplayName>Out Of Scope</b:DisplayName><b:Name>71f3d9aa-b8ef-4e54-8126-607a1d903103</b:Name><b:Value i:type="c:boolean" xmlns:c="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">false</b:Value></a:anyType><a:anyType i:type="b:StringDisplayAttribute" xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:DisplayName>Reason For Out Of Scope</b:DisplayName><b:Name>752473b6-52d4-4776-9a24-202153f7d579</b:Name><b:Value i:type="c:string" xmlns:c="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"/></a:anyType></Properties><TypeId xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">GE.A</TypeId><Height xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">561</Height><Left xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">904</Left><StrokeDashArray i:nil="true" xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts"/><StrokeThickness xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">1</StrokeThickness><Top xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">10</Top><Width xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">1086</Width></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfguidanyType><a:KeyValueOfguidanyType><a:Key>dee8ffa0-f9b8-43f6-852b-75ab2b50868e</a:Key><a:Value z:Id="i76" i:type="BorderBoundary"><GenericTypeId xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">GE.TB.B</GenericTypeId><Guid xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">dee8ffa0-f9b8-43f6-852b-75ab2b50868e</Guid><Properties xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts"><a:anyType i:type="b:HeaderDisplayAttribute" xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:DisplayName>Generic Trust Border Boundary</b:DisplayName><b:Name/><b:Value i:nil="true"/></a:anyType><a:anyType i:type="b:StringDisplayAttribute" xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:DisplayName>Name</b:DisplayName><b:Name/><b:Value i:type="c:string" xmlns:c="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">Client Machine Boundary</b:Value></a:anyType><a:anyType i:type="b:StringDisplayAttribute" xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:DisplayName>Dataflow Order</b:DisplayName><b:Name>15ccd509-98eb-49ad-b9c2-b4a2926d1780</b:Name><b:Value i:type="c:string" xmlns:c="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">0</b:Value></a:anyType></Properties><TypeId xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">GE.TB.B</TypeId><Height xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">145</Height><Left xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">605</Left><StrokeDashArray i:nil="true" xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts"/><StrokeThickness xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">1</StrokeThickness><Top xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">10</Top><Width xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">248</Width></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfguidanyType><a:KeyValueOfguidanyType><a:Key>daa3c955-cc03-4afa-9439-647fa7721953</a:Key><a:Value z:Id="i77" i:type="StencilEllipse"><GenericTypeId xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">GE.P</GenericTypeId><Guid xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">daa3c955-cc03-4afa-9439-647fa7721953</Guid><Properties xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.or