e>Out Of Scope</b:DisplayName><b:Name>71f3d9aa-b8ef-4e54-8126-607a1d903103</b:Name><b:Value i:type="c:boolean" xmlns:c="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">false</b:Value></a:anyType><a:anyType i:type="b:StringDisplayAttribute" xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:DisplayName>Reason For Out Of Scope</b:DisplayName><b:Name>752473b6-52d4-4776-9a24-202153f7d579</b:Name><b:Value i:type="c:string" xmlns:c="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"/></a:anyType></Properties><TypeId xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">GE.A</TypeId><Height xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">100</Height><Left xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">511</Left><StrokeDashArray i:nil="true" xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts"/><StrokeThickness xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">1</StrokeThickness><Top xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">1149</Top><Width xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">431</Width></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfguidanyType><a:KeyValueOfguidanyType><a:Key>980c8057-041d-48d8-abd2-3c1fff8d58cb</a:Key><a:Value z:Id="i122" i:type="StencilRectangle"><GenericTypeId xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">GE.A</GenericTypeId><Guid xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">980c8057-041d-48d8-abd2-3c1fff8d58cb</Guid><Properties xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts"><a:anyType i:type="b:HeaderDisplayAttribute" xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:DisplayName>Free Text Annotation</b:DisplayName><b:Name/><b:Value i:nil="true"/></a:anyType><a:anyType i:type="b:StringDisplayAttribute" xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:DisplayName>Name</b:DisplayName><b:Name/><b:Value i:type="c:string" xmlns:c="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">ALM Solution Bootstrap Flow&#xD;
============================&#xD;
1. Orchestration code deploys VMO via Arc for Servers extensions sequentially on each cluster node using az connectedmachine extension create. &#xD;
2. The extension installer runs as NT AUTHORITY\SYSTEM and provisions the VMOUser service account, creates the cluster storage directory (C:\ClusterStorage\...\vmo\), ACLs it to VMOUser and Administrators, creates the node-local PKI directory (C:\Program Files\ArcVM\pki\), and ACLs it similarly.&#xD;
3. The first VMO instance starts and checks cluster storage for existing PKI artifacts. Finding none, it acquires a directory lease on cluster storage (5-minute TTL) and wins the race against other VMO instances.&#xD;
4. The lease-winning VMO generates the cluster-wide PKI artifacts:&#xD;
   - A self-signed Root CA certificate and private key (ECDSA P-256, 2-year validity)&#xD;
   - A JWT signing key pair (ECDSA P-256) with the public key added to a validation pool&#xD;
   - An Overlay JWT (ES256, 45-day validity) signed with the JWT signing key&#xD;
   All artifacts are written to cluster storage with write-then-verify integrity checking. The lease is released.&#xD;
5. Each VMO instance (including the first) generates a node-specific server certificate by creating a CSR with the node's hostname, FQDN, and IP as SANs, signing it with the Root CA from cluster storage, and storing the server certificate and private key on the node-local filesystem only.&#xD;
6. Each VMO instance starts its gRPC server with TLS using its node server certificate. The server validates incoming JWTs against the signing key pool read from cluster storage.&#xD;
7. Orchestration code extracts the bootstrap bundle from cluster storage -- the Root CA public certificate (PEM) and the Overlay JWT. It validates the files are well-formed (parses PEM and JWT) with retry logic (up to 3 attempts, 10-second intervals).&#xD;
8. Orchestration code deploys the azlocal-overlay extension via Arc for Kubernetes using az k8s-extension create with --config-protected-settings containing the base64-encoded Root CA certificate pool and the Overlay JWT.&#xD;
9. The overlay extension starts on the Azure Resource Bridge and reads the bootstrap bundle from protected settings. It stores the Root CA in its local trust store and the JWT in a Kubernetes Secret (vmo-overlay-jwt in the azlocal-windows-system namespace).&#xD;
10. The overlay establishes its first authenticated connection to a VMO node:&#xD;
   - TLS handshake: overlay validates the VMO server certificate against the Root CA from the bootstrap bundle&#xD;
   - gRPC request: overlay presents the Overlay JWT in the Authorization: Bearer header&#xD;
   - VMO validates the JWT signature against its signing key pool and confirms the audience claim&#xD;
   - PowerShell Bundle is pushed to VMO &#xD;
11. On subsequent reconciliation cycles, the overlay passively detects PKI updates. During reconcileNormal(), the overlay refreshes node inventory from VMO via PowerShell invocation which includes the PKI bundle version. If the version has changed (due to CA rotation, JWT rotation, or key rotation), the overlay updates its Kubernetes Secrets with the new CA pool and JWT. After the first rotation (~22 days), the JWT exists only on-premises in Kubernetes Secrets -- Azure retains only the stale bootstrap JWT.</b:Value></a:anyType><a:anyType i:type="b:BooleanDisplayAttribute" xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:DisplayName>Out Of Scope</b:DisplayName><b:Name>71f3d9aa-b8ef-4e54-8126-607a1d903103</b:Name><b:Value i:type="c:boolean" xmlns:c="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">false</b:Value></a:anyType><a:anyType i:type="b:StringDisplayAttribute" xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:DisplayName>Reason For Out Of Scope</b:DisplayName><b:Name>752473b6-52d4-4776-9a24-202153f7d579</b:Name><b:Value i:type="c:string" xmlns:c="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"/></a:anyType></Properties><TypeId xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">GE.A</TypeId><Height xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">504</Height><Left xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">865</Left><StrokeDashArray i:nil="true" xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts"/><StrokeThickness xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">1</StrokeThickness><Top xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">10</Top><Width xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">1124</Width></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfguidanyType><a:KeyValueOfguidanyType><a:Key>9440605c-8c16-4e6e-a210-4f0fb1310c58</a:Key><a:Value z:Id="i123" i:type="BorderBoundary"><GenericTypeId xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">GE.TB.B</GenericTypeId><Guid xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">9440605c-8c16-4e6e-a210-4f0fb1310c58</Guid><Properties xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts"><a:anyType i:type="b:HeaderDisplayAttribute" xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:DisplayName>Generic Trust Border Boundary</b:DisplayName><b:Name/><b:Value i:nil="true"/></a:anyType><a:anyType i:type="b:StringDisplayAttribute" xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:DisplayName>Name</b:DisplayName><b:Name/><b:Value i:type="c:string" xmlns:c="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">Client Machine Boundary</b:Value></a:anyType><a:anyType i:type="b:StringDisplayAttribute" xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:DisplayName>Dataflow Order</b:DisplayName><b:Name>15ccd509-98eb-49ad-b9c2-b4a2926d1780</b:Name><b:Value i:type="c:string" xmlns:c="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">0</b:Value></a:anyType></Properties><TypeId xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">GE.TB.B</TypeId><Height xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">145</Height><Left xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">605</Left><StrokeDashArray i:nil="true" xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts"/><StrokeThickness xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">1</StrokeThickness><Top xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">10</Top><Width xmlns="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.Model.Abstracts">248</Width></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfguidanyType><a:KeyValueOfguidanyType><a:Key>5