's choosing.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>#17</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>1e33e6c9-2186-4386-abc4-d7bc67d91891</b:SourceGuid><b:State>AutoGenerated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>740fdbfa-c6a6-4e10-9e38-e3ddfc93973f</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>E7</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S7.196f82994-3837-4403-8b62-c48392e88d8b65e4850c-58e8-42a7-a538-f2f80a9f29746a5a274e-750b-47c3-86d4-ac3c9e0caa90</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>969edf51-46ab-4ca8-8e87-5a547bf9eebe</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>65e4850c-58e8-42a7-a538-f2f80a9f2974</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>695</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>96f82994-3837-4403-8b62-c48392e88d8b:65e4850c-58e8-42a7-a538-f2f80a9f2974:6a5a274e-750b-47c3-86d4-ac3c9e0caa90</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing of Destination Data Store etcd</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>etcd may be spoofed by an attacker and this may lead to data being written to the attacker's target instead of etcd. Consider using a standard authentication mechanism to identify the destination data store.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>From Kubernetes API Server to etcd via unnamed</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>96f82994-3837-4403-8b62-c48392e88d8b</b:SourceGuid><b:State>AutoGenerated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>6a5a274e-750b-47c3-86d4-ac3c9e0caa90</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S7.1</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>D296f82994-3837-4403-8b62-c48392e88d8b65e4850c-58e8-42a7-a538-f2f80a9f29746a5a274e-750b-47c3-86d4-ac3c9e0caa90</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>969edf51-46ab-4ca8-8e87-5a547bf9eebe</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>65e4850c-58e8-42a7-a538-f2f80a9f2974</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>696</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>96f82994-3837-4403-8b62-c48392e88d8b:65e4850c-58e8-42a7-a538-f2f80a9f2974:6a5a274e-750b-47c3-86d4-ac3c9e0caa90</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Potential Excessive Resource Consumption for Kubernetes API Server or etcd</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Denial Of Service</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Denial of Service happens when the process or a datastore is not able to service incoming requests or perform up to spec.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Does Kubernetes API Server or etcd take explicit steps to control resource consumption? Resource consumption attacks can be hard to deal with, and there are times that it makes sense to let the OS do the job. Be careful that your resource requests don't deadlock, and that they do timeout.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>From Kubernetes API Server to etcd via unnamed</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>96f82994-3837-4403-8b62-c48392e88d8b</b:SourceGuid><b:State>AutoGenerated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>6a5a274e-750b-47c3-86d4-ac3c9e0caa90</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>D2</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>D2ac946494-c283-4521-88af-e8825f19f50e75101ec4-5866-4f81-b0c4-51a15dd251773dcd02c1-c42d-4e3a-bba3-5d73cda4e6e0</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>969edf51-46ab-4ca8-8e87-5a547bf9eebe</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>75101ec4-5866-4f81-b0c4-51a15dd25177</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>729</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>ac946494-c283-4521-88af-e8825f19f50e:75101ec4-5866-4f81-b0c4-51a15dd25177:3dcd02c1-c42d-4e3a-bba3-5d73cda4e6e0</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b